//? Title: Robust comparisons and moment tests for normality
//? Subtitle: Trimmed means, rank dominance and distribution shape in C++
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare independent groups

These two samples contain ties and values far from the bulk of the group.
Yuen's test compares the centers after trimming 20 percent from each end.
Brunner–Munzel asks whether a value from the first group has a 50 percent
chance of beating a value from the second, with half credit for ties.
Cliff's delta expresses that same probability on a scale from minus one to
one. Positive differences and effects favor the first sample.

```{cpp, id=compare-robustly, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_tests.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> first{1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 8, 9, 60};
    const std::vector<double> second{-100, -2, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9, 15};
    const auto yuen = yuen_test(first, second);
    const auto bm = brunner_munzel_test(first, second);
    const auto delta = cliffs_delta(first, second);

    std::println("| Method | Estimate | 95% confidence interval | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| Yuen trimmed mean difference | {:.3f} | [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | {:.3f} |",
                 yuen.estimate1 - yuen.estimate2, yuen.conf_int_lower,
                 yuen.conf_int_upper, yuen.p_value);
    std::println("| Brunner–Munzel probability of superiority | {:.3f} | | {:.3f} |",
                 bm.estimate1, bm.p_value);
    if (const auto& bounds = delta.confidence_interval)
        std::println("| Cliff's delta | {:.3f} | [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | |",
                     delta.estimate, bounds->first, bounds->second);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Method | Estimate | 95% confidence interval | p-value |
| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |
| Yuen trimmed mean difference | 1.476 | [-1.915, 4.867] | 0.363 |
| Brunner–Munzel probability of superiority | 0.640 | | 0.242 |
| Cliff's delta | 0.280 | [-0.205, 0.764] | |
// result_end

The estimated difference and dominance both favor the first group. The two
confidence intervals include zero. The Brunner–Munzel probability of about
0.64 and Cliff's delta of about 0.28 describe the same ordering effect.

> Inspect departures from normality

The next sample has a long right tail. D'Agostino–Pearson transforms the
sample skewness and kurtosis before combining their squared scores.
Jarque–Bera weights the squared raw moment coefficients by sample size.
The component columns show how much each moment contributes to the
omnibus statistic under each method's calibration.

```{cpp, id=inspect-normality, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/normality_tests.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> sample{
        1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55, 89, 144, 233
    };
    const auto dp = dagostino_pearson_test(sample);
    const auto jb = jarque_bera_test(sample);

    std::println("| Test | Skewness | Kurtosis | Total | Asymptotic p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| D'Agostino–Pearson | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3g} |",
                 dp.skewness_contribution, dp.kurtosis_contribution, dp.statistic, dp.p_value);
    std::println("| Jarque–Bera | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3g} |",
                 jb.skewness_contribution, jb.kurtosis_contribution, jb.statistic, jb.p_value);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Skewness | Kurtosis | Total | Asymptotic p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| D'Agostino–Pearson | 15.88 | 10.48 | 26.35 | 1.89e-06 |
| Jarque–Bera | 19.04 | 19.89 | 38.93 | 3.51e-09 |
// result_end

Both tests indicate a departure from normality. The chi-square calibration
is asymptotic, especially for Jarque–Bera with a sample this small.

